Learning management systems have become a cornerstone of online and blended education, yet despite their widespread use, there is surprisingly little that truly distinguishes one system from another. Most LMS platforms offer the same core tools—quizzes, discussion forums, grade books, scheduling features, and collaborative workspaces—so much so that differences between them often come down to minor, technical details like the ability to download forum posts or record live sessions. Research has even suggested that the distinctions between various LMS products are more a matter of marketing than meaningful functionality. As a result, the real challenge in leveraging these systems effectively isn’t choosing which one to adopt, but rather ensuring successful implementation and widespread use across an institution. This shift in focus—from selection to adoption—forms the core argument of this article, which reframes the conversation around what the authors call the “other side of the LMS.”

The central problem the paper addresses is the misplaced emphasis on LMS selection, when in fact, the success of online learning depends far more on how well the system is adopted and integrated into teaching practices. The authors argue that while institutions often agonize over picking the “right” platform, market standardization has made this decision less critical. Instead, the real barriers to success lie in human and organizational factors: faculty resistance, lack of support, poor alignment with teaching styles, and insufficient training. Drawing on Everett Rogers’ Diffusion of Innovations theory, the authors examine five key attributes that influence whether an innovation like an LMS will be successfully adopted: compatibility, relative advantage, trialability, observability, and complexity.

To support adoption, institutions must first ensure the LMS is compatible with existing teaching philosophies, content areas, and institutional culture. Faculty are more likely to embrace the technology if it aligns with their pedagogical needs—for example, allowing architecture professors to annotate diagrams or enabling language instructors to incorporate audio tools. Without institutional backing and a long-term strategic vision, even the most technically sound LMS can fail to gain traction. Second, the system must offer a clear relative advantage over previous methods, whether through time savings, improved student engagement, or administrative efficiency. However, factors like excessive course preparation time, lack of compensation, and unclear intellectual property policies can undermine this advantage and deter faculty involvement.

The authors also emphasize the importance of trialability—allowing users to test the LMS on a small scale before full rollout. Starting with blended courses, where instructors can gradually integrate the system alongside face-to-face teaching, reduces risk and builds confidence. Observability plays a crucial role as well; when faculty see peers successfully using the LMS, they are more likely to adopt it themselves. Showcasing success stories, promoting communication among users, and minimizing visible technical problems all enhance user satisfaction and encourage broader uptake.

Yet implementation remains inherently complex. Many LMS platforms are not designed for easy integration, requiring significant time and technical support. Change agents—those leading the adoption process—must navigate these challenges while shielding faculty from unnecessary complexity. Strong technical support, adequate funding, and proactive problem-solving are essential to maintaining trust and momentum.

In conclusion, the authors stress that successful LMS adoption hinges not on the software itself, but on thoughtful, human-centered implementation strategies. They recommend that change agents engage in thorough research, visit institutions with successful implementations, develop a tailored adoption model, and establish clear timelines to avoid delays that increase resistance. Ultimately, the article makes a compelling case that the future of effective online learning lies not in searching for a better platform, but in improving how we use the ones we already have. By focusing on adoption, support, and cultural integration, institutions can move beyond the limitations of one-size-fits-all systems and create more flexible, responsive, and pedagogically sound learning environments.